"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[1874],{31874:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"schemaVersion":1,"locale":"en","slug":"00-vvedenie","title":"Lesson 00. Introduction: The Baseline: Preserving What Has Been Created","sourceFingerprint":"378528e47eff77c5211a91020170b2b469ba1873ea8cd58d004d272e2fc74afd","blocks":[{"id":"b001","markdown":"# Introduction. The Baseline Frontier: Preserving What Has Been Created"},{"id":"b002","markdown":"> “The central paradox of private capital: millions attempt to amass a fortune in the stock market, yet most of them would be substantially wealthier if they simply didn\'t prevent their money from quietly compounding.”"},{"id":"b003","markdown":"In the first volume of our book (*“Financial Foundation: From Accounting to Emergency Reserves”*), we closed cash flow gaps, eradicated debt bondage, built an untouchable emergency cushion, and established the regular saving of free delta."},{"id":"b004","markdown":"Now we open **Volume II — “Capital Protection: 20 Years Against Inflation”**. It is dedicated to the core challenge of the next level: how to intelligently deploy accumulated funds so that the relentless rise in prices over the coming decade does not cut their value precisely in half."},{"id":"b005","markdown":"## The Real Enemy of Capital: The Quiet Erosion of Purchasing Power"},{"id":"b006","markdown":"Let\'s look at the macroeconomic landscape of autumn 2026. On October 23, 2026, the Board of Directors of the Central Bank (CBR) held the key rate at 14% per annum. As of October 19, the regulator estimated the rate of price increases for the consumer basket at 6.8% year-over-year, with the expectation range for the end of the year standing at 7.1–7.6%. As of November 6, 2026, the key rate benchmark was fixed at 14%, while official annual inflation at the end of September was 6.35%."},{"id":"b007","markdown":"What do these percentages mean for someone holding savings in cash under a mattress or in a non-interest-bearing card account?"},{"id":"b008","markdown":"Every year, your savings silently lose around 7% of their purchasing power. If such an inflationary backdrop persists for ten years, your capital will be halved: for the same amount of cash, you will be able to buy exactly half of your usual basket of goods and services."},{"id":"b009","markdown":"| Indicator (Autumn 2026) | Value | What this means for capital |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Bank of Russia (CBR) Key Rate** (as of 23.10 and 06.11) | **14% per annum** | The cost of borrowed money is high; risk-free yield is attractive |\\n| **Annual Inflation** (September / mid-October) | **6.35% / 6.8%** | Minimum baseline of purchasing power loss through inaction |\\n| **CBR Inflation Forecast for end-2026** | **7.1–7.6%** | Savings without yield are guaranteed to depreciate by this amount |\\n| **Average Retail Account Return** (2023–2025) | **2.9% per annum** | Most active retail players lost even to a basic bank deposit |"},{"id":"b010","markdown":"In parallel, an unprecedented boom in retail investing unfolded across the country. Moscow Exchange (MOEX) statistics at the end of September 2026 revealed an impressive figure: 42.7 million citizens registered with brokerage accounts."},{"id":"b011","markdown":"However, Bank of Russia (CBR) statistics for the 2023–2025 period are sobering: the average return for retail investors was a mere **2.9% per year**. In other words, the vast majority of newcomers did not just lag behind the inflationary wave — they earned returns several times lower than a regular bank deposit, having paid brokers commissions for excessive fuss."},{"id":"b012","markdown":"## Roadmap for Volume Two: Anatomy of the Defense System"},{"id":"b013","markdown":"In this volume, we will systematically analyze instruments proven over decades and assemble a personalized investment shield:"},{"id":"b014","markdown":"1. **Chapter 1. Two Decades of Fighting Inflation.** A 2006–2026 retrospective: which asset classes protected purchasing power, and which brought hidden losses.\\n2. **Chapter 2. Anatomy of Cash Flow.** Understanding who pays for the returns generated by bank deposits, equities, physical real estate, and gold bullion.\\n3. **Chapter 3. Psychology and Risk Tolerance.** Assessing emotional resilience: how much of a drawdown can you sit through without panic selling.\\n4. **Chapter 4. The Portfolio\'s Defensive Perimeter.** Working with liquid bank deposits, government bonds (OFZ), and money market funds (LQDT).\\n5. **Chapter 5. Currency Imbalances.** Ruble devaluation risks, defensive properties of hard currencies, gold assets (including tokenized gold PAXG), and digital units of account (USDT).\\n6. **Chapter 6. Philosophy of the Systematic Investor.** Why a cold-blooded passive strategy statistically outperforms daily speculative trading.\\n7. **Chapter 7. Defense Against Imposed Solutions.** Identifying toxic offerings in the financial sector and products loaded with hidden costs.\\n8. **Chapter 8. Fee Optimization: IIS-3 and Tax Incentives.** How to claim back up to 88,000 ₽ annually and legally exempt investment gains from personal income tax.\\n9. **Chapter 9. Personal Retirement Independence.** The reality of state support versus calculating your own target capital to live off interest.\\n10. **Chapter 10. Assembling the Starter Portfolio.** Building a customized asset allocation tailored to your specific timeframe and goals."},{"id":"b015","markdown":"The practical section of this volume features two step-by-step guides:\\n* *Workshop A:* The algorithm for opening an IIS-3 account and executing your first exchange trade.\\n* *Workshop B:* Mechanics of enrolling in the Long-Term Savings Program (PDS) to maximize state co-financing."},{"id":"b016","markdown":"## Entry Control: Mandatory Prerequisites Before Getting Started"},{"id":"b017","markdown":"Proceeding to the materials in this volume is only permissible if the three basic conditions from Volume I are met:\\n* Consumer loans and microloans are fully paid off, and credit cards with predatory rates of 30–50% are eliminated.\\n* An untouchable emergency reserve covering at least 3–6 months of essential expenses has been built in a savings or deposit account.\\n* The mechanism for setting aside a portion of income every month runs on autopilot."},{"id":"b018","markdown":"Investing your last money while still carrying debt is a guaranteed path to losses: over the long haul, no classical investment instrument consistently beats a credit card interest rate of 50% per annum."},{"id":"b019","markdown":"There are no speculative stock price forecasts here, nor attempts to predict the dollar exchange rate for the next quarter: the future is stochastic, and no analyst controls it. Our goal is to examine the mechanics of financial assets, risks, and tax benefits so that you make decisions based on mathematics and common sense. All baseline data is supported by statistics from the Bank of Russia (CBR), Rosstat, the Social Fund of Russia, the Moscow Exchange (MOEX), and the Federal Tax Service."},{"id":"b020","markdown":"## \uD83E\uDDEE Margin Calculations"},{"id":"b021","markdown":"**The Effect of Capital Depreciation at an Average Annual Inflation Rate of 7%**"},{"id":"b022","markdown":"Imagine leaving the sum of **1,000,000 ₽** sitting without earning any return for 10 years, with an average price growth rate of 7% per year."},{"id":"b023","markdown":"* Formula for calculating the purchasing power of capital after $n$ years:\\n$$V = \\\\frac{S}{(1 + i)^n}$$\\nwhere $S$ is the nominal amount (1,000,000 ₽), $i$ is average annual inflation (0.07), and $n$ is the time horizon in years (10 years)."},{"id":"b024","markdown":"* Calculating the denominator:\\n$$(1 + 0{,}07)^{10} \\\\approx 1{,}967$$"},{"id":"b025","markdown":"* Final purchasing power:\\n$$V = \\\\frac{1\\\\,000\\\\,000}{1{,}967} \\\\approx 508\\\\,388 \\\\text{ ₽}$$"},{"id":"b026","markdown":"**Margin takeaway:** Over 10 years of passively sitting idle at 7% inflation, one million rubles will nominally remain a million, but it will only buy what costs **~508 000 ₽** today. The capital has lost almost 50% of its real value."}],"terms":[{"id":"inflyatsiya","term":"Inflation","definition":"an increase in the general price level that reduces the real purchasing power of money."},{"id":"klyuchevaya-stavka","term":"Key Rate","definition":"the minimum interest rate at which the Central Bank lends to commercial banks."},{"id":"podushka-bezopasnosti","term":"Emergency Fund","definition":"an untouchable reserve covering 3–6 months of basic living expenses."},{"id":"slozhnyj-procent","term":"Compound Interest","definition":"the accrual of interest on both the initial principal and the previously accumulated interest."}],"quizzes":[{"id":"q1","q":"Why did the return of retail investors on the Moscow Exchange average only 2.9% per annum in 2023–2025 despite double-digit bank deposit rates?","options":["There are no reliable instruments on the exchange with yields above 3%.","Retail investors made too many chaotic trades, paid high commissions, and attempted to speculate instead of holding assets for the long term.","The Bank of Russia established a statutory limit on the maximum return of retail brokerage accounts."],"correct":1,"explanation":"View correct answer **Correct answer: B.** Excessive trading activity, attempts to time market peaks, and panic selling led to the average retail investor earning several times less than what a conservative bank deposit would have yielded."},{"id":"q2","q":"What condition is critically important before making your first securities purchase on the stock market?","options":["Access to insider analytics from private investment channels.","Having an available amount of at least 1,000,000 ₽.","Full repayment of toxic debts and an established emergency fund covering 3–6 months of expenses."],"correct":2,"explanation":"View correct answer **Correct answer: C.** Investing while carrying high-interest debt makes no sense: paying loan interest erodes any potential portfolio return, and lacking an emergency fund forces you to sell assets at a loss at the first sign of life hardship."},{"id":"q3","q":"What will happen to the real purchasing power of savings over 10 years if they are kept in cash with an annual inflation rate of 7%?","options":["The money will completely depreciate to zero.","The purchasing power of the capital will decrease by approximately half.","The money will lose no more than 10–15% of its initial value."],"correct":1,"explanation":"View correct answer **Correct answer: B.** According to the compound interest formula, over 10 years with an annual inflation rate of 7%, the value of money decreases by a factor of $(1.07)^{10} \\\\approx 1.97$, meaning the capital loses about half of its purchasing power."}],"officialSources":[]}')}}]);